Gate 15.1 — Stage 15 (Architecture Validator) Design Proposal
Phase A — design document only. No production code, test code, or POM
changes were made while producing this proposal.
================================================================================

GROUNDING NOTES (facts checked in the current tree before proposing anything)

- Root pom.xml aggregates 5 modules: regression-core, regression-petstore-api,
  regression-jhipster, regression-nextjs-commerce, regression-mcp-server.
  Single-artifact library versions (junit-jupiter, assertj-core, gherkin,
  tag-expressions, commons-text, jna, etc.) are centrally version-managed in
  the root pom's <dependencyManagement>, even where only one module consumes
  them today (gherkin/tag-expressions are currently used only by
  regression-mcp-server). Module poms then declare the dependency unversioned.
  This is the precedent I am matching for JavaParser.
- `ModuleType` and `ModuleTypeClassifier` (package com.aqa.mcp) are
  package-private (no `public` modifier). So is `ModuleList`/`ModuleDescriptor`
  and `RepositoryRoot`. None of these types can be referenced from a new
  `com.aqa.mcp.validation` package — Java package-private access does not
  cross packages — and CLAUDE.md/this task forbid widening their visibility.
  This directly shapes the answer to item 2 below.
- `regression-mcp-server`'s current (pre-JavaParser) dependency tree, offline,
  for reference/comparison:
    com.aqa:regression-mcp-server:jar:1.0.0
    +- io.modelcontextprotocol.sdk:mcp-core:2.0.0
    |  +- jackson-annotations:2.21
    |  \- reactor-core:3.7.0 -> reactive-streams:1.0.4
    +- io.modelcontextprotocol.sdk:mcp-json-jackson2:2.0.0
    |  +- jackson-databind:2.21.2
    |  \- json-schema-validator:2.0.0 -> itu:1.14.0, jackson-dataformat-yaml:2.21.2 -> snakeyaml:2.5
    +- jackson-datatype-jsr310:2.21.2 -> jackson-core:2.21.2
    +- slf4j-api:2.0.9
    +- gherkin:28.0.0 -> messages:24.1.0
    +- tag-expressions:6.1.0
    +- slf4j-simple:2.0.9 (runtime)
    +- junit-jupiter:5.10.3 (test), assertj-core:3.27.7 (test)
  No XML/text/AST parsing library is present today besides Cucumber's Gherkin
  parser (irrelevant to Java source parsing).
- Real, currently-existing asymmetries that change how the framework_conventions
  rules must be scoped (found while reading actual module sources, not assumed):
    * regression-jhipster's UI layer (com.aqa.jhipster.ui, API_UI profile) is
      built on **Playwright** (`com.microsoft.playwright.Page`), not Selenium.
    * regression-nextjs-commerce (UI profile) is built on **Selenium**
      (`org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver`/`By`) plus its own `WaitManager`
      (com.aqa.nextjscommerce.waits.WaitManager) and `DriverSession`
      (com.aqa.nextjscommerce.driver.DriverSession) — the classes CLAUDE.md's
      "Selenium and UI safety" section describes live only in this module.
    * regression-core has `com.aqa.core.utils.WaitUtils`, not a class named
      `WaitManager`; `WaitUtils.waitFor(...)` itself legitimately calls
      `Thread.sleep(waitInterval)` inside its own polling loop.
    * regression-jhipster's `com.aqa.jhipster.ui.pages.BasePage` already
      contains an assertion (`PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat(page).hasURL(...)`
      at BasePage.java:46) inside a page object — a live, real example of
      exactly the violation validate_architecture rule (3) is meant to catch.
    * regression-nextjs-commerce has no `src/main/java` at all; all of its
      production framework code (pages, components, steps, definitions,
      driver, waits) lives under `src/test/java`. So the AST source scanner
      must walk both `src/main/java` and `src/test/java` per module — it
      cannot assume production code is always under `src/main/java`.
  These are called out explicitly below rather than papered over, because a
  profile-only rule scope (UI vs API_UI) is not precise enough for the
  Selenium-specific conventions in CLAUDE.md's "Selenium and UI safety"
  section — see item 4.

================================================================================
1. JAVAPARSER DEPENDENCY
================================================================================

Proposal: manage the version in the ROOT pom's <dependencyManagement>, and
declare the unversioned dependency directly in regression-mcp-server/pom.xml.

  Root pom.xml, new property + dependencyManagement entry (alongside the
  existing plain, non-BOM entries like commons-text/jna/assertj-core):

    <javaparser.version>3.26.4</javaparser.version>
    ...
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.github.javaparser</groupId>
        <artifactId>javaparser-core</artifactId>
        <version>${javaparser.version}</version>
    </dependency>

  regression-mcp-server/pom.xml, new unversioned <dependency>:

    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.github.javaparser</groupId>
        <artifactId>javaparser-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>

Why root, not module-local: CLAUDE.md says "use dependency and plugin
versions managed by the root pom.xml; do not introduce unmanaged versions
when a managed dependency exists." The existing precedent in this exact repo
already centrally manages libraries that (today) only regression-mcp-server
consumes — gherkin, tag-expressions, jna are all in root dependencyManagement
despite single-module usage. Declaring JavaParser's version locally in
regression-mcp-server/pom.xml instead of root would break that established
pattern for no benefit, since nothing about JavaParser is
regression-mcp-server-specific in a way that would justify a module-scoped
override (contrast with the OpenAPI Generator override case CLAUDE.md
explicitly carves out, which exists because different modules generate from
different specs — not applicable here).

Artifact choice: `javaparser-core` only (the plain AST parser/printer), not
`javaparser-symbol-solver-core`. This matches the "Context already decided"
constraint directly.

Version: I'm proposing 3.26.4 as the target but flagging it explicitly —
I do not have live Maven Central access in this session and must not guess.
The only JavaParser artifact currently cached in this machine's local
~/.m2 repository is `javaparser-core:3.15.11` (sources+jar), but its
`_remote.repositories` metadata shows it was resolved for some other,
unrelated project on this machine (May 2026, unrelated group), not this
reactor — it is a five-plus-year-old release and should not be reused just
because it happens to be present locally. Gate 15.2 (the first
implementation gate) must confirm the current latest 3.26.x/3.27.x patch
against Maven Central before adding the dependency, as its own first
verification step, rather than this design document asserting a number it
cannot verify offline.

Transitive-conflict check: javaparser-core is a self-contained, dependency-
free artifact (no runtime transitive dependencies) — it does not pull in
Guava, ASM, or anything else that could collide with the tree captured
above (Jackson 2.21.x, Netty, Reactor, SLF4J, Cucumber, JUnit, AssertJ).
This is a well-known property of the artifact, not something I can prove
from an offline tree that doesn't contain it yet; Gate 15.2 must run
`mvn -pl regression-mcp-server -am dependency:tree` again immediately after
adding the dependency and diff it against the baseline captured above as
its concrete verification step.

================================================================================
2. MODULE -> PROFILE RESOLUTION
================================================================================

The existing classification machinery (`ModuleTypeClassifier.classify`,
`ModuleType`) is reused AS-IS, unmodified, for its classification logic —
but it cannot be reused as a shared *type* across the package boundary,
because `ModuleType`/`ModuleTypeClassifier`/`ModuleList`/`ModuleDescriptor`/
`RepositoryRoot` are all package-private in `com.aqa.mcp`, and this task
(and CLAUDE.md) forbid changing their visibility.

Resolution: the validation package defines its own new, public 6-value
enum and receives the *classification outcome* as plain data (a String)
computed by the untouched existing machinery, rather than receiving the
existing type itself.

  package com.aqa.mcp.validation;

  public enum RuleProfile {
      CORE, API, UI, API_UI, MCP, TEST_ONLY;

      static List<String> schemaValues() {
          return Arrays.stream(values()).map(Enum::name).toList();
      }
  }

  public final class RuleProfileResolver {
      private RuleProfileResolver() {}

      // moduleTypeName is ModuleType.name() as computed by the existing,
      // untouched ModuleTypeClassifier — passed in as a String so this
      // package never references the package-private ModuleType type.
      public static RuleProfile resolve(String moduleTypeName) {
          return switch (moduleTypeName) {
              case "CORE" -> RuleProfile.CORE;
              case "API" -> RuleProfile.API;
              case "UI" -> RuleProfile.UI;
              case "API_UI" -> RuleProfile.API_UI;
              case "MCP" -> RuleProfile.MCP;
              case "UNKNOWN" -> RuleProfile.TEST_ONLY;
              default -> throw new ValidationException("UNKNOWN_MODULE_TYPE",
                      "Unrecognized module classification: " + moduleTypeName);
          };
      }
  }

The UNKNOWN -> TEST_ONLY mismatch, resolved explicitly (not silently):
`ModuleType.UNKNOWN` means "the classifier's fixed module-name switch didn't
recognize this module name" — it is a classification failure mode. The
"test-only" rule profile in this task's Context section means "a future
module with no production role, reserved but unused today" — a deliberate,
intentional category, not a failure mode. These are not the same concept,
but they resolve to the same practical outcome for validation purposes: in
both cases the module has no CLAUDE.md-defined production/API/UI role, so
neither the API/UI/API_UI/MCP-specific rules nor the CORE-specific rules
should fire for it, and only the module-boundary and profile-agnostic
architecture rules (layering/cycles/assertion-placement/BasePage-discipline)
apply. Mapping UNKNOWN -> TEST_ONLY therefore gives the *conservative*,
minimum-rule-set outcome in both cases, which is the safe default for an
unrecognized or intentionally-empty module. This mapping is a one-line,
reviewable, closed `switch` — not a silent assumption — and should be
called out again explicitly at Gate 15.2 review time since it's a design
judgment call, not a fact derivable from the code.

How the String actually gets produced without duplicating POM-parsing
logic: `ModuleList.forRoot(repositoryRoot)` (existing, untouched, already
reads the root pom.xml's declared `<modules>` and classifies each one via
`ModuleTypeClassifier`) already computes exactly this. The wiring classes in
`com.aqa.mcp.validation` cannot call it directly (package-private, different
package). Instead, `RegressionMcpServer.java` — which already sits in
`com.aqa.mcp` and already calls `ModuleList.forRoot(...)` for the existing
`regression_list_modules` tool — computes a `Map<String, String>` of
`moduleName -> ModuleType.name()` once and passes that plain `Map<String,
String>` (a JDK type, safe to cross the package boundary) into each of the
three new tool-wiring factory methods, alongside the `Path` of the
repository root and the `List<String>` of declared module names (exactly
the same shape `TestRunRequestValidator`'s constructor already takes today).
This means:
  - `ModuleTypeClassifier`'s classification logic is reused 100%, unmodified.
  - No visibility on any existing class changes.
  - "Derived, not hardcoded": the profile ultimately traces back to the root
    pom.xml's declared `<modules>` list read at request time, not to a
    hardcoded 5-module assumption baked into the validation package. If a
    6th module is added to the root pom tomorrow, `ModuleList`/
    `ModuleTypeClassifier` will classify it (as a known type, or UNKNOWN),
    and `RuleProfileResolver` will resolve whatever comes out — nothing in
    `com.aqa.mcp.validation` needs to change.

Open point requiring explicit sign-off before Gate 15.2/15.5 (see also the
staged plan): this task states "every existing class in com.aqa.mcp ...
stays exactly as-is, untouched." Registering the three new tools into
`McpServer.sync(...).tools(...)` and computing the `moduleName -> typeName`
map necessarily requires a small, additive edit to `RegressionMcpServer.java`
(three new constants, three new entries in the `.tools(...)` call, one small
private helper method). No existing method signature, field, or class
changes shape — this is purely additive, the same kind of change that grew
that file from its original tool set to today's 11 tools. I'm flagging this
explicitly rather than assuming it's covered by "additive is fine," because
the instruction as literally written says "untouched," and I'd rather this
be a deliberate decision than a silent interpretation.

================================================================================
3. PACKAGE STRUCTURE under com.aqa.mcp.validation
================================================================================

Mirrors the separation style already used in com.aqa.mcp.execution: a
profile/registry layer (ExecutionProfile/ExecutionProfileRegistry), a
request-validation layer (StartTestRunRequest/TestRunRequestValidator/
ValidatedTestRunRequest), a coordinator/orchestration layer
(TestRunCoordinator), immutable structured result records (RunSnapshot/
SurefireSummary/FailureArtifact), and a single typed exception
(ExecutionPlanningException). Proposed classes:

  Profile / scope resolution
  ---------------------------
  RuleProfile                  enum — the 6 rule profiles (item 2).
  ModuleProfile                record(module, profile, basePackage) — a
                                resolved module's profile tag plus its
                                derived base Java package (see item 4's
                                module-boundary rules for why basePackage
                                is derived from source, not guessed from the
                                module name).
  RuleProfileResolver           static — moduleTypeName -> RuleProfile
                                (item 2).
  ValidationException            final class extends IllegalArgumentException,
                                code() — mirrors ExecutionPlanningException/
                                RepositoryInspectionException exactly.

  Request validation (mirrors StartTestRunRequest/TestRunRequestValidator)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ValidationScopeRequest       record(String module, String profile) — raw
                                MCP tool arguments, both nullable.
  ValidatedValidationScope     record(List<String> modules) — the resolved
                                set of module names in scope after applying
                                the module/profile filters against the
                                declared-modules list.
  ValidationScopeValidator     final class — validate(ValidationScopeRequest,
                                List<String> declaredModules) ->
                                ValidatedValidationScope; mirrors
                                TestRunRequestValidator's shape and
                                constructor-injected declaredModules list.

  AST parsing entry point (mirrors FeatureDiscovery's file-walk + parse)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SourceUnit                    record(String module, String relativePath,
                                CompilationUnit unit) — one parsed .java
                                file; relativePath is reactor-root-relative
                                (e.g. "regression-jhipster/src/main/java/
                                com/aqa/jhipster/ui/pages/BasePage.java"),
                                mirroring how FeatureDiscovery.ParsedFeature
                                carries a module-root-relative path.
  JavaSourceScanner              final class — scan(Path repositoryRoot,
                                String module) -> List<SourceUnit>; walks
                                both src/main/java and src/test/java under
                                the module (bounded file count/size,
                                symlink-containment-checked, deterministic
                                sort — the same shape as
                                FeatureDiscovery.files()/validate()), parses
                                each file with JavaParser's StaticJavaParser.
                                A file that fails to parse produces a
                                ValidationException("SOURCE_PARSE_ERROR", ...)
                                rather than being silently skipped, mirroring
                                FeatureDiscovery's GHERKIN_PARSE_ERROR
                                handling.
  BasePackages                  final class — static derive(List<SourceUnit>)
                                -> String; computes a module's base Java
                                package as the longest common package prefix
                                across that module's own parsed
                                PackageDeclaration nodes. Used to populate
                                ModuleProfile.basePackage without hardcoding
                                a naming convention.

  Rule model (item 4)
  ---------------------------
  ValidationRule                interface — id(), Set<RuleProfile>
                                profiles(), List<Violation>
                                evaluate(EvaluationContext).
  EvaluationContext              record(String module, RuleProfile profile,
                                List<SourceUnit> moduleSources,
                                List<ModuleProfile> reactorModules) — a
                                module's own parsed sources plus read-only
                                metadata (name/profile/basePackage only, no
                                parsed AST) for every other declared module,
                                which is what module-boundary rules need to
                                check cross-module imports without paying
                                to parse the whole reactor for every rule.
  Violation                     record(String ruleId, String module,
                                String file, int line, String message) —
                                bounded/structured, mirrors
                                SurefireSummary.FailureRecord's shape rather
                                than a free-text string.

  Rule-set implementations (one per tool, mirrors execution's per-concern
  classes rather than one god class)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ModuleBoundaryRules            final class — static List<ValidationRule>
                                all(); the fixed rule list backing
                                regression_validate_module_boundaries.
  FrameworkConventionRules        final class — same shape, backing
                                regression_validate_framework_conventions.
  ArchitectureRules               final class — same shape, backing
                                regression_validate_architecture.

  Result model (mirrors RunSnapshot/SurefireSummary's aggregate shape)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ModuleValidationResult         record(String module, RuleProfile profile,
                                List<String> rulesApplied,
                                List<Violation> violations, boolean
                                truncated).
  ValidationReport               record(List<ModuleValidationResult>
                                modules) — top-level per-tool result,
                                mirrors moduleListOutputSchema's "modules"
                                wrapping shape already used by
                                regression_list_modules.

  MCP tool wiring (one class per tool; mirrors RegressionMcpServer's
  per-tool static factory methods, just extracted since these three live in
  the new package)
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ModuleBoundariesTool            final class — static SyncToolSpecification
                                tool(Path repositoryRoot, List<String>
                                declaredModules, Map<String,String>
                                moduleTypeByName).
  FrameworkConventionsTool         same shape.
  ArchitectureTool                 same shape.

  Each takes only JDK types (Path, List<String>, Map<String,String>) —
  never the package-private com.aqa.mcp types — so RegressionMcpServer.java
  can call e.g. `ModuleBoundariesTool.tool(repositoryRoot.path(),
  declaredModuleNames, moduleTypeByName)` as a same-shape sibling call next
  to its existing `listModulesTool(repositoryRoot)` line.

================================================================================
4. RULE MODEL
================================================================================

Interface:

  public interface ValidationRule {
      String id();
      Set<RuleProfile> profiles();
      List<Violation> evaluate(EvaluationContext context);
  }

A rule declares which profiles it applies to; the tool-wiring class (not the
rule) is responsible for only invoking a rule when
`!Collections.disjoint(rule.profiles(), Set.of(context.profile()))`. Module-
boundary rules declare `EnumSet.allOf(RuleProfile.class)` since dependency-
direction rules are not profile-specific.

If a module later carries multiple profile tags: `EvaluationContext.profile()`
(singular) would become `Set<RuleProfile> profiles()` (plural), and the
membership test in the tool-wiring class would change from `profiles().
contains(context.profile())` to `!Collections.disjoint(rule.profiles(),
context.profiles())`. The `ValidationRule` interface itself would not need to
change at all — it already expresses its applicability as a Set, and rules
never read `context.profile()` directly inside `evaluate()` (they receive
already-scoped sources and only use profile for the tool-wiring dispatch
decision), so this is a contained, one-record change plus one call-site
change in each of the three tool-wiring classes, not a rule-by-rule rewrite.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
validate_module_boundaries — concrete rules (direct from CLAUDE.md text)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  MOD-001  Sibling product modules must not depend on one another.
           regression-petstore-api, regression-jhipster and
           regression-nextjs-commerce must remain independent of one
           another ("Do not create dependencies between sibling product
           modules"). Detection: for each of these three modules' own
           SourceUnits, inspect every ImportDeclaration's qualified name;
           flag any import whose prefix matches another sibling module's
           derived basePackage (from EvaluationContext.reactorModules()).

  MOD-002  regression-nextjs-commerce must not gain API clients, API
           services, or API scenario code ("Do not add API clients, API
           services, or API scenarios to regression-nextjs-commerce").
           Detection: for regression-nextjs-commerce's SourceUnits only,
           flag any ImportDeclaration referencing a known REST/HTTP client
           library already used elsewhere in the reactor for API testing
           (e.g. jakarta.ws.rs.*, org.glassfish.jersey.*) — these types
           appearing at all inside this module's own compilation units is
           itself the signal, independent of which package declares them.

  MOD-003  regression-mcp-server must not depend on regression-core or any
           product module. Detection: for regression-mcp-server's own
           SourceUnits (i.e. this rule fires when context.module() equals
           "regression-mcp-server"), flag any ImportDeclaration whose
           prefix matches any other reactor module's derived basePackage.

  MOD-004  regression-core must not depend on any product module or on
           regression-mcp-server (the inverse-direction companion to
           MOD-003, implied by "Product modules inherit from it" / core is
           the shared base — a dependency from core outward to a consumer
           would itself be a layering inversion). Detection: symmetric to
           MOD-003, scoped to context.module().equals("regression-core").

  Note: "module boundaries" are fundamentally a dependency-declaration
  concept (pom.xml <dependency> elements), and JavaParser cannot read a
  pom.xml. The rules above are deliberately framed as *source-level* import
  checks rather than pom-level dependency-graph checks, because (a) an
  illegal compile-time dependency can't produce a matching import in the
  first place without the pom already declaring it — so a pom-level check
  would only ever tell you "yes/no," not where — and (b) the import-level
  check gives an actionable file/line Violation, consistent with this
  tool's structured-record contract. Whether a complementary, coarser
  pom-level "does module X declare module Y as a Maven dependency" check
  should also be added (catching the case where a dependency is declared
  but never yet imported) is an open question for Gate 15.3, not decided
  here — it would need its own small secure-XML reader in the validation
  package (the existing one in ModuleList is package-private and
  unreachable, same constraint as item 2).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
validate_framework_conventions — concrete rules (direct from CLAUDE.md text)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  IMPORTANT SCOPING CAVEAT found while grounding this proposal: CLAUDE.md's
  "Selenium and UI safety" section describes conventions (By-locator fields,
  WaitManager, DriverSession, no static mutable WebDriver) that, in the
  actual current codebase, are true of regression-nextjs-commerce (UI
  profile, genuinely Selenium-based) but NOT of regression-jhipster's UI
  layer (API_UI profile, genuinely Playwright-based — it uses
  com.microsoft.playwright.Page/Locator, not org.openqa.selenium.WebDriver/
  By). A rule scoped only by RuleProfile ∈ {UI, API_UI} would misfire on
  regression-jhipster for the Selenium-specific rules below. Each
  Selenium-specific rule is therefore additionally gated on "this
  compilation unit imports org.openqa.selenium.*" as a precondition, not on
  profile alone. Whether regression-jhipster's Playwright-based UI code
  should get its own parallel set of technology-appropriate conventions
  rules is an open question for Gate 15.4 — flagging it, not deciding it.

  FC-001  Selenium locators declared as `private static final By` fields
           at the top of the class (not inline in methods, not non-static,
           not non-final). Scope: UI/API_UI-profile compilation units that
           import org.openqa.selenium.By. Detection: for each ClassOrInter-
           faceDeclaration, find FieldDeclaration nodes whose element type
           is `By`; flag any that is missing `static` or `final` or is not
           `private`. A companion check flags `By.<factoryMethod>(...)`
           MethodCallExpr occurring outside of a field initializer (i.e.
           inline in a method body) as an inline-locator violation.

  FC-002  No `Thread.sleep`; use the module's wait abstraction instead.
           Detection: any MethodCallExpr named "sleep" whose scope is a
           NameExpr "Thread", anywhere in a UI/API_UI-profile module's
           sources — EXCEPT inside a fixed, explicit allow-list of the
           wait-abstraction's own implementation files (e.g.
           com/aqa/core/utils/WaitUtils.java,
           com/aqa/nextjscommerce/waits/WaitManager.java). This allow-list
           is a small, reviewable, explicit exception list, not a general
           suppression mechanism — WaitUtils.java itself legitimately calls
           Thread.sleep inside its own polling loop today, so a rule
           without this exemption would immediately false-positive on
           existing, correct code.

  FC-003  Constructor injection for collaborators; avoid service locators /
           hidden mutable dependencies. Detection: for classes in
           definitions/steps/pages/services/components/clients packages,
           flag any non-final, non-constructor-assigned field whose type is
           itself one of this reactor's own collaborator types (heuristic:
           declared type's package starts with a reactor basePackage) —
           i.e. a mutable field of a collaborator type is a candidate
           violation; a `static` field of such a type is always a
           violation regardless of finality (overlaps with the "no static
           mutable" rules below).

  FC-004  Records for small immutable value objects / scenario input /
           snapshots / expected state; mutable Beans only when framework
           binding, mapping, staged construction, or nested mutable data
           requires them. Detection: this one is inherently heuristic and
           lower-precision than the others — flag a ClassOrInterfaceDeclara-
           tion (not already a `record`) whose every field is `private
           final`, whose only methods are a single all-args constructor and
           plain getters (no other business logic), as a "candidate value
           object not declared as a record" finding. Given the heuristic
           nature, this rule should ship as advisory-only in its first cut
           (Gate 15.4 decision, flagged here rather than assumed) rather
           than being held to the same precision bar as the structural
           rules above.

  FC-005  DriverSession / page objects must remain scenario-local — no
           static mutable WebDriver, browser, page-object, or scenario-
           state instances. Detection: any FieldDeclaration with modifier
           `static` (and not `final` + an immutable/constant initializer)
           whose type is `WebDriver` (or a subtype import from
           org.openqa.selenium), or whose type's simple name ends in
           "Page"/"Component" (page-object heuristic), or whose type is the
           module's own scenario-context type (e.g.
           CommerceScenarioContext) — scoped to UI/API_UI profiles, with
           the same Selenium-import precondition as FC-001/FC-002 for the
           WebDriver-specific half of this rule.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
validate_architecture — the 4 agreed rules, with concrete AST detection
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

  ARCH-001  Layering: Gherkin -> definitions -> steps -> pages/services ->
            components/clients. Detection: derive each class's "layer" from
            its package's last non-numeric segment (definitions/steps/
            pages/services/components/clients — a closed, known vocabulary,
            not free-form), then for every MethodCallExpr and every field/
            parameter type reference inside a class, resolve the referenced
            class's own layer the same way (via ImportDeclaration, since no
            symbol solver is in scope yet) and flag any reference where the
            calling layer is *later* in the fixed order than the referenced
            layer's declared position would allow going *backward* (e.g. a
            class under .../pages/... importing something under
            .../steps/...). Concretely this only needs a fixed total order
            over the 5 layer names and a same-module import-prefix check —
            no cross-file resolution beyond import statements.

  ARCH-002  No dependency cycles between packages within a module.
            Detection: build a directed graph per module where each node is
            a package (derived from each SourceUnit's PackageDeclaration)
            and each edge A->B is added whenever any class in package A has
            an ImportDeclaration into package B; run a standard cycle
            detection (DFS with a recursion stack) over that graph; report
            one Violation per package participating in a detected cycle,
            with `message` listing the cycle's package sequence.

  ARCH-003  Assertions only in steps/test-layer, never in page objects.
            Detection: for every class under a `.../pages/...` package,
            scan for MethodCallExpr nodes whose resolved-by-import scope is
            a known assertion entry point — `org.assertj.core.api.
            Assertions.assertThat`, `com.microsoft.playwright.assertions.
            PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat`, `org.junit.jupiter.api.
            Assertions.assert*` — via a static-import or qualified-name
            match against the file's ImportDeclarations (no symbol solver
            needed since these are well-known, closed entry points, not
            arbitrary user code). This rule already has a real,
            pre-existing positive match in the current tree —
            regression-jhipster's ui/pages/BasePage.java:46 calls
            `PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat(page).hasURL(...)` inside
            `assertUrlContains`, a page-object method — which is useful as
            a concrete worked example for this rule's fixture design (see
            item 6) but is NOT being changed in this Phase-A proposal.

  ARCH-004  BasePage must not become a general utility class. Detection:
            for each class named exactly `BasePage` (per-module — the
            codebase currently has two independent BasePage classes, one
            per UI-capable module, not one shared canonical class, so this
            rule must not assume a single FQCN), flag it if its public/
            protected method count exceeds a fixed threshold, or if it
            declares a method whose name/behavior isn't obviously page-
            navigation/lifecycle related (heuristic: methods not named/
            prefixed with a closed vocabulary like navigateTo/waitUntil*/
            byDataCy/assert* would need a second look) — this is the
            softest of the four rules and, like FC-004, should ship
            advisory-only in its first cut; a hard structural rule here
            risks false positives on legitimate small helper methods, and
            getting the threshold right needs real-repo calibration during
            Gate 15.5, not a number picked in this design document.

================================================================================
5. MCP TOOL SCHEMAS
================================================================================

All three tools follow the existing closed-schema, oneOf{success,failure}
structured envelope already used by every tool in RegressionMcpServer.java
(the `structuredOutputSchema`/`moduleErrorResult` pattern), with
`additionalProperties: false` throughout and read-only ToolAnnotations
(readOnlyHint=true, destructiveHint=false, idempotentHint=true,
openWorldHint=false — these are pure inspection tools, same annotation
shape as regression_list_modules/regression_get_framework_overview).

Input schema (identical shape for all three tools):

  {
    "type": "object", "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
      "module": {"type": "string"},
      "profile": {"type": "string", "enum": [
          "CORE", "API", "UI", "API_UI", "MCP", "TEST_ONLY"]}
    }
  }

  Both fields optional. Neither given -> validate every declared module.
  Only "module" given -> validate that one module (INVALID_ARGUMENTS if not
  a declared module, mirroring UNKNOWN_MODULE handling already used by
  FeatureDiscovery). Only "profile" given -> validate every declared module
  whose resolved RuleProfile matches. Both given -> both must agree
  (INVALID_ARGUMENTS if the named module's resolved profile isn't the
  given profile) — this mirrors moduleArgument()'s existing "arguments must
  be known/consistent" validation style in RegressionMcpServer.java.

Violation record schema (shared by all three tools' output):

  {
    "type": "object", "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": ["ruleId", "module", "file", "line", "message"],
    "properties": {
      "ruleId": {"type": "string"},
      "module": {"type": "string"},
      "file": {"type": "string"},
      "line": {"type": "integer"},
      "message": {"type": "string"}
    }
  }

Per-module result schema (shared):

  {
    "type": "object", "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": ["module", "profile", "rulesApplied", "violations", "truncated"],
    "properties": {
      "module": {"type": "string"},
      "profile": {"type": "string", "enum": [...RuleProfile values...]},
      "rulesApplied": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}},
      "violations": {"type": "array", "items": <violation schema above>},
      "truncated": {"type": "boolean"}
    }
  }

Output schema (structuredOutputSchema-wrapped, same for all three tools):

  data: { "modules": {"type": "array", "items": <per-module result schema>} }

Error envelope: identical to every existing tool — {"status":"error",
"error":{"code":..., "message":...}} — with codes drawn from a small closed
set: INVALID_ARGUMENTS, UNKNOWN_MODULE, SOURCE_PARSE_ERROR,
UNKNOWN_MODULE_TYPE (item 2), mirroring RepositoryInspectionException's
existing code vocabulary (POM_ERROR, FEATURE_PATH_VIOLATION, UNKNOWN_MODULE,
GHERKIN_PARSE_ERROR, ...).

Tool names/descriptions (matching the existing SCREAMING_SNAKE constant +
one-sentence description convention in RegressionMcpServer.java):

  VALIDATE_MODULE_BOUNDARIES_TOOL_NAME = "regression_validate_module_boundaries"
    "Checks declared reactor module source against the fixed sibling-module
     and layering-boundary rules in the repository's module-boundary policy."
  VALIDATE_FRAMEWORK_CONVENTIONS_TOOL_NAME = "regression_validate_framework_conventions"
    "Checks a module's source against its resolved rule profile's framework
     coding-convention rules (locators, waits, injection, value objects,
     scenario-local state)."
  VALIDATE_ARCHITECTURE_TOOL_NAME = "regression_validate_architecture"
    "Checks a module's source against the fixed layering, dependency-cycle,
     assertion-placement, and BasePage-discipline architecture rules."

================================================================================
6. TEST STRATEGY
================================================================================

Two tiers, matching what already exists in this test tree rather than
inventing a new convention:

  Tier 1 — pure rule-logic unit tests (no filesystem at all). Every
  ValidationRule gets exactly one positive (violating) and one negative
  (compliant) JUnit test, each parsing an inline Java 21 text-block source
  snippet directly via JavaParser's StaticJavaParser.parse(String), wrapping
  the resulting CompilationUnit in a single SourceUnit, building a minimal
  EvaluationContext (module name + profile + that one SourceUnit + an empty
  or single-entry reactorModules list, whichever the rule needs), and
  calling rule.evaluate(context) directly — asserting either exactly the
  expected Violation (ruleId + line) or an empty list. This mirrors
  TestRunRequestValidatorTest's pattern of exercising the validator directly
  with in-memory inputs, and FeatureDiscoveryTest's use of inline text
  blocks for fixture content — just without needing @TempDir, since a
  single parsed compilation unit doesn't need a real directory tree.
  Concretely, ARCH-003's negative/positive pair can be lifted almost
  verbatim from the real BasePage.java found during grounding (item 4) —
  a positive fixture snippet that keeps the offending
  `PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat(...)` call inside a `pages` package
  class, and a negative fixture that moves the same assertion into a
  `steps`-package class instead.

  Tier 2 — scanner and tool-wiring integration tests (need a real directory
  tree). JavaSourceScannerTest and each of the three *ToolTest classes use
  @TempDir exactly like FeatureDiscoveryTest/ModuleListTest already do:
  build a synthetic fake reactor root at test time (a root pom.xml
  declaring 2-3 fake <module> entries, each with its own tiny pom.xml and a
  handful of .java files written as text blocks under src/main/java and/or
  src/test/java), then exercise JavaSourceScanner.scan(...) or the tool's
  SyncToolSpecification callHandler against that synthetic root. This is
  the existing project convention for exactly this kind of test (no
  modification of real regression-core/regression-petstore-api/
  regression-jhipster/regression-nextjs-commerce source is ever needed, and
  no new static fixture resource files are introduced — everything is
  synthesized at test time, consistent with how FeatureDiscoveryTest and
  ModuleListTest already work).

  Additional dedicated unit tests mirroring existing sibling tests:
    RuleProfileResolverTest        mirrors ModuleTypeClassifier's implicit
                                  coverage in ModuleListTest — one test per
                                  ModuleType-name -> RuleProfile mapping,
                                  including the UNKNOWN -> TEST_ONLY case.
    ValidationScopeValidatorTest    mirrors TestRunRequestValidatorTest's
                                  shape (valid module, invalid module,
                                  profile/module mismatch, no filters).
    BasePackagesTest                unit tests for the common-prefix
                                  derivation, including a single-file module
                                  and a multi-package module.

  MCP-level tests: each *Tool class gets a schema/contract-level test in the
  style of RegressionMcpServerContractTest (input/output schema shape
  assertions, INVALID_ARGUMENTS on unknown module) rather than a full STDIO
  process-lifecycle test like RegressionMcpServerStdioIntegrationTest — these
  three tools are synchronous, read-only, closed-world inspection tools like
  the four existing overview/listModules/features/scenarios tools, not like
  the execution tools that need process/lifecycle coverage.

