--------------------------------------------------------------------------
WOULD ANY OF THESE FAIL TO COMPILE FROM com.aqa.mcp IN GATES 15.3-15.5?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Answer, under the Gate 15.1 design as accepted: NO — none of the 15
classes are called directly from com.aqa.mcp at all, so nothing here is a
blocker today, and none of the 4 package-private members need to change.

Why: the accepted Gate 15.1 design places the three *Tool wiring classes
(ModuleBoundariesTool, FrameworkConventionsTool, ArchitectureTool) INSIDE
com.aqa.mcp.validation, not inside com.aqa.mcp. RegressionMcpServer.java's
only planned cross-package calls (Gate 15.3-15.5, not yet made) are to
those three classes' static factory methods:

  XxxTool.tool(Path repositoryRoot, List<String> declaredModules,
      Map<String,String> moduleTypeByName) -> SyncToolSpecification

which take only JDK types (Path, List<String>, Map<String,String>) that
RegressionMcpServer.java already builds itself from its own existing,
untouched, package-private ModuleList/ModuleTypeClassifier machinery, and
return only the MCP SDK's SyncToolSpecification. RegressionMcpServer.java
never references RuleProfile, ModuleProfile, RuleProfileResolver,
ValidationException, ValidationScopeRequest, ValidatedValidationScope,
ValidationScopeValidator, SourceUnit, JavaSourceScanner, BasePackages,
Violation, EvaluationContext, ValidationRule, ModuleValidationResult, or
ValidationReport by name anywhere in the plan — all of that traffic stays
inside com.aqa.mcp.validation, between the rule-set classes and the *Tool
classes that will be added in Gates 15.3-15.5, which are in the same
package as the 15 chassis classes audited here.

Given that, the 4 package-private members found are each only ever called
from within com.aqa.mcp.validation, under both the current code and the
planned Gate 15.3-15.5 additions:
  - RuleProfile.schemaValues() would be called by a *Tool class (same
    package) to build a tool's JSON input schema's profile enum list.
  - ValidationException's 3-arg constructor is only ever called by
    JavaSourceScanner (same package) to wrap an IOException as a cause;
    no caller outside the package constructs a ValidationException — an
    outside caller (if one ever needed to exist) would only ever catch
    one and read `code()`/`getMessage()`, both of which are already public.
  - JavaSourceScanner.MAX_JAVA_FILE_BYTES/MAX_JAVA_FILES are bound
    constants referenced only by JavaSourceScanner itself and by
    JavaSourceScannerTest (same package, exactly mirroring how
    FeatureDiscovery.MAX_FEATURE_FILE_BYTES/MAX_FEATURE_FILES are already
    package-private and referenced the same way from FeatureDiscoveryTest
    today).

Caveat, stated explicitly rather than assumed silently: this "no blocker"
conclusion is conditional on Gate 15.3 actually placing the *Tool wiring
classes inside com.aqa.mcp.validation, per the accepted design. If that
placement changes in Gate 15.3 (for example, if RegressionMcpServer.java
were instead made to construct an EvaluationContext or call
BasePackages.derive(...) itself, or to build a tool's JSON schema itself
using RuleProfile.schemaValues()), the visibility picture above would need
to be re-checked at that time — but as of right now, with zero *Tool
classes yet created, nothing in the 15 audited classes blocks Gate 15.3.

No code was changed to produce this report.
