Gate 15.3 — regression_validate_module_boundaries End-to-End Implementation
First gate touching RegressionMcpServer.java (plus one additional,
user-approved touch to RegressionMcpServerStdioIntegrationTest.java —
see Phase A finding 4 below).
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE A — verification before writing code
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. RegressionMcpServer.java's exact tool-registration shape, read fresh:

   Every tool is a `static SyncToolSpecification xTool(...)` method that
   builds `Tool.builder(NAME, inputSchema()).description(...)
   .annotations(...).outputSchema(...).build()` wrapped in
   `SyncToolSpecification.builder().tool(...).callHandler((exchange,
   request) -> { try { ... successResult(...) } catch (X exception) {
   return errorResult(exception.code(), exception.getMessage()); } })
   .build()`. All 11 are registered in exactly one place:

     static void createServer(RepositoryRoot repositoryRoot, TestRunCoordinator coordinator) {
         Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(coordinator::close, "regression-mcp-shutdown"));
         InputStream input = new CloseAwareInputStream(System.in, coordinator::close);
         McpServer.sync(new StdioServerTransportProvider(McpJsonDefaults.getMapper(), input, System.out))
                 .serverInfo(SERVER_NAME, SERVER_VERSION)
                 .instructions(INSTRUCTIONS)
                 .capabilities(ServerCapabilities.builder().tools(false).build())
                 .tools(overviewTool(repositoryRoot), listModulesTool(repositoryRoot), featureListTool(repositoryRoot),
                         scenarioListTool(repositoryRoot), startTestRunTool(coordinator), getTestRunTool(coordinator),
                         cancelTestRunTool(coordinator), testSummaryTool(coordinator), failureSummaryTool(coordinator),
                         failureArtifactsTool(coordinator), readFailureArtifactTool(coordinator))
                 .build();
     }

   `regression_list_modules` calls `ModuleList.forRoot(RepositoryRootResolver
   .resolve(repositoryRoot.path()))` FRESH, inside its own callHandler, on
   every single request — not once at startup. Every other read-only tool
   (overview, features, scenarios) does the identical fresh-per-request
   resolve. This turned out to be load-bearing (see finding 2).

2. How the Map<String,String> gets built without touching ModuleList/
   ModuleType/ModuleTypeClassifier's visibility — REVISED from the
   Gate 15.1/15.2 plan after a concrete test-driven discovery:

   The original plan (compute the map ONCE inside createServer and pass it
   as a fixed Map<String,String> parameter into
   ModuleBoundariesTool.tool(...), per the literal signature given) is
   UNSAFE. RegressionMcpServerStdioIntegrationTest's existing test
   `returnsStructuredPomErrorsWithoutExposingFixturePathsAndRemainsUsable`
   starts a real server subprocess against a deliberately malformed root
   pom.xml (`"<project><modules>"`, unclosed) via `createServer(...)`, and
   asserts the server still completes MCP `initialize`, that
   `regression_list_modules` fails per-call with a structured `POM_ERROR`,
   and that `regression_get_framework_overview` still succeeds afterward —
   i.e. the server must keep starting and stay usable even when the pom is
   malformed. If `ModuleList.forRoot(...)` were called eagerly inside
   `createServer`, that exact malformed-pom fixture would throw during
   server startup (caught by `main()`'s existing `catch
   (IllegalArgumentException) { System.exit(2); }`), and this existing
   test would fail because the process would never reach a servable state.

   Fix: `ModuleBoundariesTool.tool(Path repositoryRoot, Supplier<Map<String,
   String>> moduleTypeByNameSupplier)` — the supplier is invoked once PER
   REQUEST, inside the tool's own callHandler, not at registration time.
   RegressionMcpServer.java's `createServer` now adds exactly this:

     .tools(..., ModuleBoundariesTool.tool(repositoryRoot.path(), () -> moduleTypeByName(repositoryRoot)))
             .build();
     }

     private static Map<String, String> moduleTypeByName(RepositoryRoot repositoryRoot) {
         Map<String, String> types = new java.util.LinkedHashMap<>();
         for (ModuleDescriptor module : ModuleList.forRoot(RepositoryRootResolver.resolve(repositoryRoot.path())).modules()) {
             types.put(module.name(), module.type().name());
         }
         return java.util.Collections.unmodifiableMap(types);
     }

   This reuses ModuleList/ModuleType/ModuleTypeClassifier exactly as-is
   (zero visibility change), matches every other tool's per-request
   freshness behavior exactly, and defers all pom-parsing risk to request
   time — a malformed pom.xml now fails only the tools that need module
   data (regression_list_modules, regression_list_features,
   regression_list_scenarios, and now regression_validate_module_boundaries),
   never server startup. Returning `Collections.unmodifiableMap(...)` over
   the LinkedHashMap (not `Map.copyOf(...)`) was deliberate: `Map.copyOf`
   does not guarantee iteration-order preservation, and declaration order
   needs to survive into `ValidatedValidationScope.modules()` for
   deterministic, order-preserving output.

3. Base package prefixes, re-verified fresh against the current tree
   (unchanged since Gate 15.1's grounding): regression-core -> com.aqa.core,
   regression-petstore-api -> com.aqa.petstore, regression-jhipster ->
   com.aqa.jhipster, regression-nextjs-commerce -> com.aqa.nextjscommerce,
   regression-mcp-server -> com.aqa.mcp. Also checked real cross-module
   imports today: zero sibling-to-sibling product-module imports exist
   (no MOD-001 violations today), regression-nextjs-commerce imports zero
   jakarta.ws.rs/org.glassfish.jersey (no MOD-002 violations today),
   regression-core and regression-mcp-server import zero other-module
   packages (no MOD-003/MOD-004 violations today). The real reactor is
   clean; all fixtures used for testing are synthetic, per the accepted
   design.

4. BLOCKING FINDING, resolved with the user before Phase B: adding a 12th
   tool to the shared `.tools(...)` call breaks two existing,
   supposedly-untouchable tests in RegressionMcpServerStdioIntegrationTest.java:
   `assertToolList` hardcodes `tools.size() == 11`, and
   `assertExecutionToolContracts` hardcodes an exact
   `containsExactlyInAnyOrder(...)` of the 11 current tool-name constants —
   both called from 2 existing tests
   (servesDiscoveryToolsOverStdioAndTerminatesWhenTheClientDisconnects,
   servesControlledExecutionToolsOverStdioAndCleansRunOnEof). There is no
   way to register a 12th real tool without this count drifting. I raised
   this to the user rather than deciding unilaterally; the user chose to
   authorize updating those two hardcoded assertions (11->12, plus adding
   RegressionMcpServer.VALIDATE_MODULE_BOUNDARIES_TOOL_NAME to the expected
   name set) as the minimal, correct fix, rather than skipping real
   registration or shipping a known-red suite. This is therefore a second,
   explicitly user-approved touch to an existing file, beyond the single
   file the task text had pre-authorized.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE B — implementation
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. RULES — com/aqa/mcp/validation/ModuleBoundaryRules.java (package-private
   final class; its only consumer, ModuleBoundariesTool, is in the same
   package, so no public surface is needed). `static List<ValidationRule>
   all()` returns 4 private static nested ValidationRule implementations,
   each `profiles() = EnumSet.allOf(RuleProfile.class)`:

     MOD-001 SiblingModuleIndependence — fires only when context.module()
       is one of the 3 sibling product modules; flags any ImportDeclaration
       whose name matches another SIBLING's derived basePackage (importing
       regression-core is explicitly allowed — core is not a sibling).
     MOD-002 CommerceHasNoApiClients — fires only for
       regression-nextjs-commerce; flags any import starting with
       "jakarta.ws.rs." or "org.glassfish.jersey.".
     MOD-003 McpServerIndependence — fires only for regression-mcp-server;
       flags any import matching ANY other declared module's basePackage
       (self-imports of com.aqa.mcp.* are allowed).
     MOD-004 CoreIndependence — fires only for regression-core; flags any
       import matching any OTHER declared module's basePackage.

   Shared helper `forbiddenModuleImports(context, forbiddenModules, ruleId,
   messagePrefix)` walks every SourceUnit's `CompilationUnit.getImports()`,
   and for each ImportDeclaration checks `matchesBasePackage(importName,
   candidate.basePackage())` — a package-segment-aware prefix match
   (`importName.equals(basePackage) || importName.startsWith(basePackage +
   ".")`, explicitly guarding against a blank/undetermined basePackage
   matching everything). Violation line numbers come from
   `importDeclaration.getBegin().map(position -> position.line).orElse(1)`.

   Known, deliberate characteristic: `rulesApplied` in a module's result
   always lists all 4 rule ids whenever that module's profile is in scope
   (since `profiles()` is EnumSet.allOf for every rule) — MOD-002/003/004's
   own additional module-name gate is internal to each rule's `evaluate()`,
   not exposed as a separate "not applicable" state. A module outside a
   given rule's module-name scope shows that rule as "applied" with zero
   violations contributed, not omitted from rulesApplied. Documented here
   as intended, not a defect.

2. TOOL WIRING — com/aqa/mcp/validation/ModuleBoundariesTool.java (public
   final class, since RegressionMcpServer.java must call it cross-package):

     public static final String TOOL_NAME = "regression_validate_module_boundaries";
     public static SyncToolSpecification tool(Path repositoryRoot, Supplier<Map<String, String>> moduleTypeByNameSupplier)

   callHandler flow: parse raw MCP arguments into ValidationScopeRequest ->
   invoke the supplier (fresh per request, see finding 2) ->
   ValidationScopeValidator.validate(request, declaredModules,
   moduleTypeByName) -> scan EVERY declared module's sources via
   JavaSourceScanner.scan(...) (not just in-scope modules — cross-module
   boundary checks need every module's basePackage regardless of the
   requested scope, so this tool always reads the whole reactor's Java
   source; a parse failure in an out-of-scope module therefore still fails
   the request with SOURCE_PARSE_ERROR — a deliberate fail-closed choice
   for a tool whose entire purpose is cross-module correctness) -> derive
   each module's basePackage via BasePackages.derive(...) -> build one
   ModuleProfile per declared module (reactorModules) -> for each in-scope
   module, build an EvaluationContext, run every ModuleBoundaryRules rule
   whose profiles() contains that module's resolved RuleProfile, collect
   Violations -> assemble ModuleValidationResult (truncated always false —
   no truncation logic needed at this rule count) -> ValidationReport.

   Self-contained schema/serialization helpers (successResult, errorResult,
   serialize, structured schema builders) are local to this class — they
   cannot reuse RegressionMcpServer's private helpers (different class,
   private is class-scoped, not package-scoped), so this is necessary,
   expected duplication of the same shape shown in the Gate 15.1 proposal.

   Input schema: {module?: string, profile?: string enum of the 6
   RuleProfile names}, additionalProperties: false, neither required.
   Output: the same closed oneOf{success,failure} envelope shape as every
   existing tool; data.modules[] each {module, profile, rulesApplied[],
   violations[], truncated}; each violation {ruleId, module, file, line,
   message}. Error codes surfaced: INVALID_ARGUMENTS, UNKNOWN_MODULE,
   SOURCE_PARSE_ERROR, UNKNOWN_MODULE_TYPE (all pre-existing
   ValidationException codes from Gate 15.2's chassis, none new).

3. REGISTRATION — the diff to RegressionMcpServer.java:
   - New import: `import com.aqa.mcp.validation.ModuleBoundariesTool;`
   - New constant, placed after READ_FAILURE_ARTIFACT_TOOL_NAME:
     `static final String VALIDATE_MODULE_BOUNDARIES_TOOL_NAME = ModuleBoundariesTool.TOOL_NAME;`
     (sourced from ModuleBoundariesTool.TOOL_NAME rather than a second
     hardcoded literal, so the tool's name has one owner and cannot drift
     between the two files — RegressionMcpServer.java still gets its own
     named constant, matching every other tool's convention, it just isn't
     a second copy of the literal string.)
   - `.tools(...)` gained one more argument:
     `ModuleBoundariesTool.tool(repositoryRoot.path(), () -> moduleTypeByName(repositoryRoot))`
   - New private helper `moduleTypeByName(RepositoryRoot)` (shown in
     finding 2), placed directly after the modified `createServer` method.
   No existing tool's method body, order, or behavior was touched — the
   11 existing `.tools(...)` arguments are untouched and appear in their
   original order, with the new one appended last.

   Second, user-approved diff to RegressionMcpServerStdioIntegrationTest.java
   (finding 4): `assertToolList`'s `isEqualTo(11)` -> `isEqualTo(12)`;
   `assertExecutionToolContracts`'s `containsExactlyInAnyOrder(...)` gained
   a trailing `RegressionMcpServer.VALIDATE_MODULE_BOUNDARIES_TOOL_NAME`.
   Nothing else in that file changed.

4. TESTS — 7 new classes, 36 new test methods total:

   a) Deferred Gate 15.2 invariant tests (compact-constructor valid/null/
      blank coverage, mirroring SurefireSummary-style record invariant
      testing already implicit elsewhere in this codebase):
        ViolationTest (4), ModuleProfileTest (4), EvaluationContextTest (5,
        including defensive-copy-of-mutable-input and null-list-defaulting),
        ModuleValidationResultTest (5, same shape).

   b) ModuleBoundaryRulesTest (Tier 1, 10 tests) — one positive + one
      negative inline text-block fixture per rule (MOD-001..004, 8 tests),
