GATE 15.5 -- Phase A (read-only inspect/propose): regression_validate_architecture
================================================================================

TASK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Phase A only (no implementation). Propose ArchitectureRules covering
ARCH-001 (layering), ARCH-002 (no package cycles), ARCH-003 (no assertions
in page objects), ARCH-004 (BasePage discipline, advisory), grounded in
direct inspection of the real reactor. No production code touched.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. LAYER-IDENTIFICATION CONVENTION -- CONFIRMED BY DIRECT INSPECTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Listed every .java file's package across regression-jhipster,
regression-nextjs-commerce, regression-petstore-api, and regression-core,
and read representative definitions classes (LoginDefinitions.java,
ProductDefinitions.java, AuthDefinitions.java, S3Definitions.java,
GeneralDefinitions.java) directly.

CONFIRMED: the layer is reliably identifiable from the package's last
segment alone -- every reactor module that has a layered structure uses
exactly these segment names, consistently, with no exceptions found:
`definitions`, `steps`, `pages`, `services`, `components`. No package
literally named `clients` exists anywhere in the reactor today (Jersey/
HTTP transport lives under `services`, e.g. regression-core's
GeneralApiService) -- `clients` remains part of the closed vocabulary per
CLAUDE.md's text but is currently an empty layer with zero real files, not
a rule-breaking gap.

Definitions classes are consistently records or small final classes,
constructor-injected with exactly the layer below them (a Steps class, or
in regression-core's case sometimes a Controller/Steps combination), and
their @Given/@When/@Then-annotated methods only ever delegate to that
injected collaborator -- confirmed clean across every definitions class
read. Proposed detection signal for ARCH-001: for a class whose package's
last segment is `definitions`, flag a MethodCallExpr whose immediate
scope is a field of the definitions class itself, where that field's
declared type's package (via ImportDeclaration, no symbol solver) ends in
`.pages`, `.services`, or `.components`, or whose declared type is a
Selenium `By`/Playwright `Locator`/`Page` type directly -- i.e. only
single-hop, directly-typed collaborator fields are examined, exactly
mirroring FC-001-PW's own field-type-based approach rather than resolving
method return types.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. ARCH-001 -- A REAL DETECTION-LIMITATION FINDING (needs your decision)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Found a genuine case while reading every real definitions class:
regression-core's S3Definitions.get(...) calls
`s3Steps.s3ServiceActions().getObject(...)` -- i.e. it reaches through the
Steps layer's own accessor to obtain a services-layer object
(S3ServiceActions) and then calls the actual business action on it
directly, rather than the Steps class exposing a method that performs the
action itself. This is a two-hop chained call: `s3Steps` is a legitimate
`.steps`-typed field (not itself a violation), but `.s3ServiceActions()`
returns a `.services`-package type, and detecting that requires resolving
a method's *return type*, not just a field's *declared type* -- which is
exactly the kind of thing the accepted design says a Symbol Solver should
only be added for once "genuinely needed for a specific rule," not
speculatively.

This is a real, concrete case demonstrating that limitation, not a
hypothetical one. Two honest options: (a) ship ARCH-001 with single-hop,
field-type-only detection (matches every other rule's no-symbol-solver
design so far) -- this real S3Definitions case will NOT be flagged, a
known, accepted gap; or (b) add a Symbol Solver now, since this gate has
now produced the first concrete demonstrated need the accepted design
anticipated. Flagging this for your decision rather than picking one
silently, per the same standard applied to FC-001-PW in Gate 15.4 -- this
is a real scope-and-precision tradeoff, not a naming/tactical detail.

Separately, and NOT proposed for inclusion without your authorization:
regression-core's GeneralDefinitions.java calls `assertThat(...)`
directly inside multiple @Then-annotated methods -- a definitions-layer
class making assertions directly. CLAUDE.md's "Architecture" section
says "Do not place browser calls, locators, assertions, services, or
business branching in definitions," so this is a real, pre-existing
violation of a documented rule -- but none of the four already-accepted
ARCH-001..004 rules as scoped actually cover "no assertions in
definitions" (ARCH-003 covers page objects only). Noting this only as an
informational finding, exactly as Gate 15.4 Phase A noted the pre-existing
BasePage.java:46 assertion without proposing to fix it or expand scope --
not proposing to broaden ARCH-001/003 to cover it without your say-so.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. ARCH-002 -- package-dependency-cycle detection, confirmed scope
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Confirmed scope: package-level cycles within one module only, never
class-level, never cross-module (cross-module boundaries are MOD-001..
004's job, already shipped in Gate 15.3, not this rule's).

Proposed construction: for each module, build a directed graph where each
node is a package derived from each SourceUnit's own
CompilationUnit.getPackageDeclaration() (the file's actual package, not
BasePackages's derived common-prefix base package -- that record already
exists and is reused only to decide which imports are "internal to this
module" in the next step). For every class in package A, for every
ImportDeclaration whose target starts with the module's own base package
(from BasePackages.derive, filtering out JDK/library/other-module
imports), add an edge A -> B where B is the imported class's own package
(the import's name minus its last segment). Run a standard DFS-with-
recursion-stack cycle detection over the resulting graph per module;
report one Violation per package participating in a detected cycle, with
`message` listing the full cycle's package sequence -- directly reusing
Gate 15.1's original design, unchanged, since nothing in the codebase
inspection this gate contradicts it.

This rule is genuinely module-structure-agnostic (it doesn't care whether
a module has definitions/steps/pages/services/components at all) and so
applies meaningfully and uniformly to every module including
regression-core and regression-mcp-server, unlike ARCH-001/003 (see
section 5). Not exhaustively verified for zero real cycles across every
module in Phase A (unlike MOD/FC's grep-verified clean baselines) --
proposing this be established by Phase B's own tool-level test against
the real reactor rather than a manual Phase A pass, since cycle detection
across ~140 real source files is exactly what the rule/tool itself is
for; flagging this so it isn't silently assumed clean.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. ARCH-003 -- re-verified against CURRENT file contents, not memory
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Re-read every page/component file in both UI-capable modules fresh (not
from Gate 15.4's report) and grepped for `assertThat(` / `Assertions.assert`
across both modules' pages and components packages directly:

  regression-jhipster ui.pages (4 files, 14 call sites):
    BasePage.java:46 (assertThat(page).hasURL(...))
    LoginPage.java:43,44,45,62 (assertThat(...).isVisible(), x1 more)
    BankAccountPage.java:45,46,58,59,74
    BankAccountFormPage.java:43,44,45,46
  regression-jhipster ui.components (3 files, 4 call sites):
    BaseComponent.java:20, DataTableComponent.java:32,42,
    NavigationBar.java:22
  regression-jhipster ui.pages HomePage.java: re-read fresh, confirmed
    ZERO assertions (delegates to NavigationBar.assertAuthenticated()
    instead) -- not every jhipster page has this issue, HomePage is clean.
  regression-nextjs-commerce pages (all files) and components (all files):
    re-read/grepped fresh, ZERO assertion calls anywhere in either
    package.

CONFIRMED total: 18 real, pre-existing ARCH-003 violation call sites
across 7 files, ALL in regression-jhipster (API_UI/Playwright), ZERO in
regression-nextjs-commerce (UI/Selenium). This is a materially different
situation from every rule shipped in Gates 15.3/15.4: MOD-001..004 and
FC-001..005 all have a clean real-reactor baseline (synthetic @TempDir
fixtures only prove the rules fire). ARCH-003 will NOT have a clean
baseline -- calling regression_validate_architecture against the real
reactor today will immediately surface 18 real violations concentrated
entirely in one module. This is presumably the validator doing exactly
its job (surfacing a real, already-known, deliberately-not-fixed-yet
issue -- Gate 15.4's Phase A already flagged BasePage.java:46 as a
"useful real fixture reference... deliberately NOT fixed, out of scope
for the validator project"), so this is reported as a confirmed finding
for your awareness rather than a blocking ambiguity -- but flagging it
explicitly since it changes what "shipping ARCH-003" concretely means in
practice (real, visible findings from day one, not a theoretical rule).

Detection signal, gated the same way FC-001/FC-001-PW were: for a class
whose package's last segment is `pages` or `components`, flag a
MethodCallExpr named `assertThat` (or any JUnit `Assertions.assert*`
static-import call) whose invocation is reachable via one of the known,
closed entry-point imports -- `org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat`
/ `AssertionsForClassTypes.assertThat`, `com.microsoft.playwright.
assertions.PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat`, `org.junit.jupiter.api.
Assertions.assert*` -- matched against the file's own static
ImportDeclarations, exactly as Gate 15.1's original design proposed. No
symbol solver needed since these are well-known, closed entry points.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. ARCH-004 -- BasePage discipline, calibrated against BOTH real classes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Confirmed regression-nextjs-commerce DOES have its own BasePage despite
having no src/main/java: regression-nextjs-commerce/src/test/java/com/aqa/
nextjscommerce/pages/BasePage.java exists (production framework code
living under src/test/java, consistent with the already-known fact that
100% of this module's framework code lives there). Two independent
BasePage classes confirmed, per-module, not a single shared FQCN --
matches Gate 15.1's original note.

Re-read both BasePage.java files fresh in full and counted their
public/protected methods (excluding constructors and private helpers):

  regression-jhipster BasePage.java -- 4 public/protected methods:
    navigateTo(String) [protected], waitUntilLoaded() [public abstract],
    byDataCy(String) [protected], assertUrlContains(String) [protected].

  regression-nextjs-commerce BasePage.java -- 7 public/protected methods:
    navigateTo(String) [protected], waitUntilLoaded() [public],
    waitForPageContent() [protected abstract], header() [public],
    cart() [public], currentUrl() [public], title() [public].

DESIGN FINDING -- Gate 15.1's original two-pronged heuristic (method-count
threshold OR a closed method-name-vocabulary check like
navigateTo/waitUntil*/byDataCy/assert*) does not hold up cleanly against
the real nextjs-commerce BasePage: header(), cart(), currentUrl(), and
title() are all legitimate, narrow accessor responsibilities (giving
subclasses typed access to shared page chrome and page metadata) but none
of them match that literal name-prefix vocabulary, so the vocabulary-half
of the heuristic would flag 4 of 7 methods on a real BasePage that has no
actual utility-class-creep problem -- a real false-positive risk against
today's clean code, discovered by direct calibration rather than assumed.

PROPOSAL: drop the name-vocabulary half entirely and use a method-count-
only threshold. Real data: jhipster=4, nextjs-commerce=7. A threshold of
"more than 8 public/protected methods" passes both real classes cleanly
(4 <= 8, 7 <= 8) while still being low enough to catch genuine bloat (a
BasePage with, say, 15+ public methods). This is a real design deviation
from Gate 15.1's original two-pronged proposal, grounded in fresh
calibration data -- flagging it for your confirmation the same way
FC-001-PW's adaptation was flagged in Gate 15.4, since ARCH-004 is
advisory-only but its *detection signal* is still a real design choice,
not a tactical detail.

