PART 1.3 -- Code-consistency re-inspection of regression-mcp-server
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Scope covered: all 61 main-source files and 47 test-source files across
`com.aqa.mcp`, `com.aqa.mcp.execution`, and `com.aqa.mcp.validation`
(Stage 15). No TODO/FIXME/XXX/@Deprecated markers exist anywhere in the
module's source (checked directly) -- no leftover scaffolding markers from
Stage 13-15.

Findings, rated as requested:

1. [MODERATE -- worth doing before v1.0] Near-total structural duplication
   across the three Stage 15 `*Tool` classes: `ModuleBoundariesTool.java`
   (179 lines), `FrameworkConventionsTool.java` (193 lines), and
   `ArchitectureTool.java` (192 lines). Directly diffed `ModuleBoundariesTool`
   against `ArchitectureTool`: `evaluate(...)`, `parseRequest(...)`,
   `violationOutput(...)`, `inputSchema()`, `violationSchema()`,
   `readOnlyAnnotations()`, `successResult(...)`, `errorResult(...)`, and
   `serialize(...)` are byte-for-byte identical or near-identical (only the
   rule-source class name and, in `ArchitectureTool`'s case, the
   advisory-violations partitioning differ). This is roughly 120-140
   duplicated lines x3. STAGE_15_PROGRESS.md's own Gate 15.5 notes already
   acknowledge this explicitly ("ArchitectureTool follows the identical
   per-request Supplier wiring pattern... no new wiring pattern is
   introduced"), so this was a known, deliberate, incremental choice at each
   gate rather than an oversight -- but with all three tools now shipped, a
   shared `ValidationToolSupport`-style helper (schema builders, result
   envelopes, the scope-resolve-then-evaluate loop) would remove the
   duplication without touching any one tool's distinct behavior. This is a
   genuine "worth doing before v1.0" candidate given how much of the file
   count is now pure repetition, not a "nice to have" of taste. NOT
   implemented -- proposal only, awaiting your authorization if you want
   Stage 16 (or a dedicated gate) to include it.

2. [TRIVIAL/COSMETIC] `RegressionMcpServer.java`'s `errorResult(code,
   message)` is a one-line passthrough to `moduleErrorResult(code,
   message)` -- same body, two names, both used across the file for
   entirely generic (not module-specific) errors. `moduleErrorResult` reads
   as a name left over from when only the module-list tool needed an error
   envelope; the method has since become the generic error-envelope builder
   for every tool in the file. Cosmetic naming leftover, zero behavioral
   impact.

3. [TRIVIAL/COSMETIC] `RegressionMcpServer.java` mixes two formatting
   styles: verbose multi-line tool-builder methods (e.g. `overviewTool`,
   `listModulesTool`) versus very dense single-line-chained methods (e.g.
   `startTestRunTool`, `runActionTool`, most of the schema builders from
   line ~380 onward). This makes the file's own internal style
   inconsistent, though each individual method is still readable in
   isolation. No functional issue; a readability/consistency nit only.

4. [TRIVIAL/COSMETIC] Test-naming mismatch: `execution/SurefireSummaryStoreTest.java`
   exercises `RunStore`'s `summary(...)` method (there is no
   `SurefireSummaryStore` class anywhere in main source -- confirmed by
   listing all 44 main-source files). The class under test is `RunStore`;
   the test class name suggests a `SurefireSummaryStore` type that does not
   exist. Purely a naming artifact, not a coverage gap -- the test itself is
   substantive and correctly scoped to `RunStore`.

5. [INFORMATIONAL, not a proposal] Three structurally identical (`code` +
   `message`, extends `IllegalArgumentException`) exception classes exist,
   one per package: `com.aqa.mcp.RepositoryInspectionException`,
   `com.aqa.mcp.execution.ExecutionPlanningException`,
   `com.aqa.mcp.validation.ValidationException`. Directly compared all
   three; they are near-identical (~18-20 lines each). Unlike finding 1,
   this triplication reads as consistent with the same deliberate
   package-isolation rationale already documented and accepted at Gate
   15.0.5 (rejecting a package reorg specifically to avoid widening
   cross-package visibility) -- a shared exception type would need to live
   in a shared public location, which is exactly the kind of coupling that
   gate already declined. Not proposing any change; noting it only so it's
   visible it was checked, not missed.

6. [Explicitly checked, nothing found] Package structure sanity: `com.aqa.mcp`
   (root discovery/overview types), `com.aqa.mcp.execution` (21+ execution/
   run-store classes), and `com.aqa.mcp.validation` (21 Stage 15 classes)
   remain cleanly separated with no new cross-package leakage introduced
   since Gate 15.5's own visibility audit. No dead/unreferenced classes were
   found in the validation package inventory (matches STAGE_15_PROGRESS.md's
   documented 21 main / 20 test class inventory exactly -- recounted
   directly via file listing, same numbers).

No findings beyond the six above and the three already-known/accepted debt
items were manufactured. Nothing else in the module reads as leftover
scaffolding, dead code, or a structural problem.

